
  Before I can design anything, one fork decides nearly everything downstream.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what "across sessions" means — across browser restarts on one machine, or across devices for a logged-in
  user. That single answer determines whether this is a ~50-line client module or a feature that requires standing up a
  backend and a real auth system (the current [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that returns [38;5;153m{success: true}[39m for any input, so there is
  no user identity to key preferences to yet).

  - [1mBrowser-local ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m)[0m — buys you persistence across tabs and browser restarts with zero backend and zero
    dependencies. Costs: preferences are per-device and per-browser, lost on cache clear, and readable by any script on
    the page. Rules out cross-device sync until you add a server, though a local store is straightforward to later use
    as a cache in front of one.
  - [1mServer-backed per account[0m — buys real cross-device persistence tied to a user. Costs: a backend, a database, a
    preferences API, and real authentication — none of which exist here. This is several times the scope of the other
    two and would need its own decomposition.
  - [1mNode CLI config file[0m — if "sessions" means runs of [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m, this is a config file under the user's home
    directory. Different codebase half entirely, unrelated to the login page.

  [1mMy recommendation: browser-local.[0m The webapp is the only part of this repo with anything resembling a "session," and a
  server-backed store isn't a preferences feature — it's a backend project with preferences on top. A local store is
  cheap to change later and can become the client cache for a synced store if you ever add one.
